<p>
  Gives each user a revocable credential to put in build tooling instead of their password.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>How it is used.</strong> The token is an HTTP basic auth pair: the user name is the owner's Nexus user id
  and the password is a generated secret. Any client that speaks basic auth works without modification, which covers
  Maven, Docker, npm and curl.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Why it matters with an external directory.</strong> When accounts come from LDAP or an identity provider,
  a password in <code>settings.xml</code> is the user's directory password, usable against every other system that
  trusts that directory. A token is useless anywhere but this server, so a leaked settings file no longer widens
  beyond it.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Revocation.</strong> A token can be reset by its owner at any time, which invalidates the previous value
  immediately. Tokens also stop working as soon as their owner's account is disabled or removed, because the owner's
  status is rechecked on every authentication.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Requiring tokens.</strong> With <em>Require User Tokens for Repository Access</em> set, repository traffic
  authenticated with a plain password is refused. Leave it off until tokens have been distributed, or existing
  builds will begin failing as soon as the capability is saved.
</p>
